     8	## A12.1 Bounded termination
     9	
    10	**Registered text changed.**
    11	- A2.3 (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:288-289`): "termination is a contract obligation on the implementation, not a
    12	  proven liveness property."
    13	- A2.1 (`phase-0-prereg-amendment-2.md:135-138`): "An attempt whose lag exceeds S may be refused; a chain-valid
    14	  artifact of such an attempt remains cryptographically valid and evidentially admissible but carries no protocol
    15	  standing."
    16	
    17	**Why.** The issuer ends an attempt only on complete negative evidence (lifecycle decision rev 5.3.1). A dependency
    18	that fails for good (calendars that never answer, explorers that never agree) leaves a request open indefinitely:
    19	the obligation of A2.3 is unmet for that case, and an open request is a resource an attacker can hold (the author,
    20	2026-10-30: "Why wouldn't you just define a failure after some finite period of time (the FLP work-around)?
    21	Otherwise, I'd worry that you've created a denial-of-service attack vector.").
    22	
    23	**Registered change.**
    24	1. **Bound.** Every request reaches a terminal state no later than **acceptance + 14 days** of service wall-clock
    25	   time (L_term). 14 days = the nominal budget N × (δ + S) = 12 days (A2.3) plus 2 days of operating allowance.
    26	2. **Outcome.** A request not COMPLETE at L_term ends `REFUSED / EVIDENCE_UNAVAILABLE`. Every open attempt ends with
    27	   it; no attempt is allocated after it.
    28	3. **Standing.** An attempt ended this way is an attempt whose lag exceeded S under A2.1: any artifact of it carries
    29	   no protocol standing, even if evidence later shows it was timely. A2.1's permission ("may be refused") becomes,
    30	   at L_term, an obligation.
    31	4. **What it costs, stated.** A request that would eventually have qualified can be refused. That is a liveness
    32	   loss, never a safety loss: a refusal never yields a receipt, so no false receipt can result. Clients resubmit.
    33	5. **What it does not change.** Safety properties, the latch rule, the expiry-on-complete-negative rule before
    34	   L_term, the window's signing stop, and the verifier.
    35	6. **Implementation duty.** The `EVIDENCE_UNAVAILABLE` ending, gated in code since 2026-10-30 pending this decision,
    36	   is enabled with L_term; its TLA+ model (`formal/tla/issuer-lifecycle/`) gains the bound and checks that every
    37	   request terminates by L_term under the failure model.
